/*
Definition for Node
class Node
{
    int data;
    Node left;
    Node right;

    Node(int data)
    {
        this.data = data;
        left = null;
        right = null;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public int getSize(Node root) {

        if(root == null) return 0;                                              // Base case: null node has size 0
        
        return (getSize(root.left) + getSize(root.right) + 1);                  // Recursively sum left + right + current node
    }
}

//////////////////////// DRY RUN /////////////////////////////

Example: Binary Tree

      1
     / \
    2   3

getSize(1)
  ├─ getInstance(2)
  │   ├─ getSize(null) → 0
  │   ├─ getSize(null) → 0
  │   └─ 0 + 0 + 1 = 1
  ├─ getSize(3)
  │   ├─ getSize(null) → 0
  │   ├─ getSize(null) → 0
  │   └─ 0 + 0 + 1 = 1
  └─ 1 + 1 + 1 = 3

Output: 3

//////////////////////// QUESTION ////////////////////////////

Size of Binary Tree
Given the root node of a binary tree, return the total number of nodes present in the tree.
The size of a binary tree is defined as the count of all its nodes.

Examples:

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Input:
      1
     / \
    2   3

Output: 3
Explanation: 3 nodes in the tree

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Input:
        1
       /
      2
     /
    3
   / \
  4   5
 /
6

Output: 6
Explanation: 6 nodes in the tree

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Constraints:
• 1 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10^5
• 1 ≤ node->data ≤ 10^5

